IMPORT prelude

§ `Set operators — Phase 2: the overloads, and the two ANDs side by side`

-- The acceptance test of SET-OPERATORS-SPEC.md §5, as amended by §16:
-- term-level set union is spelled UNION explicitly; sentence-level AND on
-- BOOLEANs stays conjunction. The AND/OR-on-sets overloads were REMOVED
-- 2026-07-28 — a second __AND__/__OR__ candidate made typechecking
-- exponential (base 3) in AND/OR chain depth (smucclaw/l4-ide#929), so
-- the type-directed dispatch of the same token now stops at the boolean
-- reading, and the union reading is written in its own word.

§§ `Term-level union: spelled UNION`

`new yorkers`   MEANS setFromList (LIST "alice", "bob")
`new jerseyans` MEANS setFromList (LIST "carol")

-- "residents of New York and New Jersey may apply": either state
-- qualifies. The drafter resolves the source text's "and" to UNION
-- explicitly; AND on two SETs is a type error (see
-- not-ok/tc/set-and-unoverloaded.l4).
`eligible` MEANS `new yorkers` UNION `new jerseyans`

#EVAL setSize `eligible`
#EVAL "carol" `is in` `eligible`

§§ `Sentence-level AND: conjunction, as always`

`is cruel`   MEANS TRUE
`is unusual` MEANS FALSE

-- "cruel and unusual punishments": must be both.
`violates 8th amendment` MEANS `is cruel` AND `is unusual`

#EVAL `violates 8th amendment`

§§ `PLUS and MINUS: bare, precedence-correct — and not a group`

a1 MEANS setFromList (LIST 1, 2, 3)
b1 MEANS setFromList (LIST 2, 3, 4)

#EVAL setSize (a1 PLUS b1)
#EVAL setSize (a1 MINUS b1)
-- unparenthesized chain: left-associates at arithmetic precedence
#EVAL setSize (a1 PLUS b1 MINUS b1)
-- the semilattice trap, demonstrated: (a1 PLUS b1) MINUS b1 is NOT a1
#EVAL setToList (a1 PLUS b1 MINUS b1)
-- numeric dispatch is untouched
#EVAL 5 MINUS 2
#EVAL 5 PLUS 2

§§ `Extensional equality`

-- order and duplicates are invisible under `set equals`.
-- (Bare EQUALS on two SETs is deliberately an ambiguity error — see
-- not-ok/tc/set-equals-ambiguous.l4 and prelude `Sets` notes.)
#EVAL (setFromList (LIST 2, 1, 1)) `set equals` (setFromList (LIST 1, 2))
#EVAL (setFromList (LIST 1)) `set equals` (setFromList (LIST 1, 2))
-- numeric and string EQUALS untouched
#EVAL 42 EQUALS 42
#EVAL "a" EQUALS "b"
